1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,578
A packet disappears, and the sender hears
nothing. Was the message

2
00:00:07,578 --> 00:00:15,156
lost, or just the reply? That uncertainty is
enough to cause

3
00:00:15,156 --> 00:00:22,733
duplicates, endless waiting, or missing data.
Let's build a way out.

4
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:28,317
Read this picture as a timeline. The sender is
on the

5
00:00:28,317 --> 00:00:33,900
left, the receiver on the right, and time
moves downward. Sloping

6
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:39,483
arrows cross the distance; returning arrows
carry acknowledgments, small messages that

7
00:00:39,483 --> 00:00:45,067
report progress.

8
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:50,750
Start with the simplest rule: send one packet,
keep a copy,

9
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:56,433
and wait for confirmation before sending
another. This is stop-and-wait. Keeping

10
00:00:56,433 --> 00:01:02,117
the copy gives the sender something to use if
the attempt

11
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:07,800
fails.

12
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:13,483
We need one precise convention. An ACK names
the next packet

13
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:19,167
expected. ACK one confirms packet zero; ACK
four confirms zero through

14
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:24,850
three. That number points to the first missing
position in the

15
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:30,533
ordered sequence.

16
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:36,117
Notice the empty space after transmission. The
packet is still travelling,

17
00:01:36,117 --> 00:01:41,700
and its acknowledgment must travel back. The
sender could have more

18
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:47,283
data ready, yet our rule leaves it idle until
that round

19
00:01:47,283 --> 00:01:52,867
trip finishes.

20
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:58,658
Two delays explain this gap. Transmission time
measures putting the bits

21
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:04,450
onto the link; propagation time measures their
travel. A faster transmitter

22
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:10,242
reduces one delay without making the signal
itself cross the distance

23
00:02:10,242 --> 00:02:16,033
instantly.

24
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:23,611
Here, transmission takes one millisecond and
round-trip propagation takes twenty. Ignoring

25
00:02:23,611 --> 00:02:31,189
other overhead, only one millisecond in
twenty-one is spent sending useful

26
00:02:31,189 --> 00:02:38,767
data: about four point eight percent. The
waiting dominates the work.

27
00:02:38,767 --> 00:02:44,458
Now lose the packet. No acknowledgment
returns, but silence doesn't reveal

28
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:50,150
the cause. The data might be missing, its
acknowledgment might be

29
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:55,842
missing, or either could simply be late.
Waiting forever cannot resolve

30
00:02:55,842 --> 00:03:01,533
that uncertainty.

31
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:07,217
A timer gives the sender a decision point.
When enough time

32
00:03:07,217 --> 00:03:12,900
passes without confirmation, recovery begins.
Notice what it hasn't established: a

33
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:18,583
timeout is a reason to retry, not proof that
the original

34
00:03:18,583 --> 00:03:24,267
packet vanished.

35
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:29,842
The saved copy is sent again with the same
sequence number.

36
00:03:29,842 --> 00:03:35,417
This attempt reaches the receiver, and ACK one
returns. With progress

37
00:03:35,417 --> 00:03:40,992
confirmed, the sender can release that copy
and prepare the next

38
00:03:40,992 --> 00:03:46,567
packet.

39
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:52,042
Change just one event: deliver the data, but
lose its acknowledgment.

40
00:03:52,042 --> 00:03:57,517
The sender sees the same silence and retries.
Now a perfectly

41
00:03:57,517 --> 00:04:02,992
reasonable recovery creates a second arrival.
How can the receiver avoid

42
00:04:02,992 --> 00:04:08,467
delivering the contents twice?

43
00:04:08,467 --> 00:04:14,150
The sequence number answers that question. The
receiver expects one, so

44
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:19,833
another zero is a duplicate. It repeats ACK
one without delivering

45
00:04:19,833 --> 00:04:25,517
the data again. Repeating transmission doesn't
have to mean repeating delivery

46
00:04:25,517 --> 00:04:31,200
to the application.

47
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:37,000
For basic stop-and-wait, alternating zero and
one can distinguish successive packets

48
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,800
under suitable initialization and old-
duplicate assumptions. Our larger numbers make
the

49
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:48,600
examples easier to track, but sequence
information needn't always be a

50
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:54,400
large counter.

51
00:04:54,400 --> 00:05:00,192
Set the timer too short and late packets
trigger unnecessary retries.

52
00:05:00,192 --> 00:05:05,983
Set it too long and actual losses cause long
stalls. This

53
00:05:05,983 --> 00:05:11,775
delayed acknowledgment exposes the balance.
Real protocols can estimate delays and

54
00:05:11,775 --> 00:05:17,567
adapt their timers.

55
00:05:17,567 --> 00:05:23,358
Can we use the waiting time? Allow four
unconfirmed packets instead

56
00:05:23,358 --> 00:05:29,150
of one. The sending window limits how much
data may be

57
00:05:29,150 --> 00:05:34,942
outstanding. It changes the sender's
permission to proceed, without adding another

58
00:05:34,942 --> 00:05:40,733
physical link.

59
00:05:40,733 --> 00:05:46,417
Now zero, one, two, and three follow each
other into the

60
00:05:46,417 --> 00:05:52,100
network. Earlier packets travel while later
ones leave, and acknowledgments return

61
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:57,783
behind them. The same link carries more useful
work because we

62
00:05:57,783 --> 00:06:03,467
stopped waiting after every packet.

63
00:06:03,467 --> 00:06:08,942
ACK three confirms the complete run through
packet two. That's cumulative

64
00:06:08,942 --> 00:06:14,417
acknowledgment: one reply can report progress
across several packets. The boundary

65
00:06:14,417 --> 00:06:19,892
can advance only through consecutive received
data, leaving a gap as

66
00:06:19,892 --> 00:06:25,367
the stopping point.

67
00:06:25,367 --> 00:06:31,050
In this separate example, ACK two confirms
zero and one. The

68
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:36,733
window moves forward two positions, allowing
new packets at its far

69
00:06:36,733 --> 00:06:42,417
edge. Follow the boundary: the allowed range
moves, while its size

70
00:06:42,417 --> 00:06:48,100
stays four.

71
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:53,892
Now lose packet one after sending zero through
three. Zero arrives,

72
00:06:53,892 --> 00:06:59,683
so the receiver asks for one. We pause new
sends here

73
00:06:59,683 --> 00:07:05,475
to isolate the problem: the sender still lacks
confirmation for one,

74
00:07:05,475 --> 00:07:11,267
two, and three.

75
00:07:11,267 --> 00:07:16,742
Our basic Go-Back-N receiver discards two and
three because one is

76
00:07:16,742 --> 00:07:22,217
missing. Each later arrival prompts ACK one
again. Those packets reached

77
00:07:22,217 --> 00:07:27,692
the destination, but this protocol hasn't
accepted them into the ordered

78
00:07:27,692 --> 00:07:33,167
sequence.

79
00:07:33,167 --> 00:07:41,033
When packet one's timer expires, the sender
retries one, two, and

80
00:07:41,033 --> 00:07:48,900
three. Zero remains confirmed. This explains
Go-Back-N's extra work after a

81
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:56,767
loss: recovery repeats the outstanding
sequence starting at the gap.

82
00:07:56,767 --> 00:08:02,450
The repeated packets now arrive in order. ACK
four advances the

83
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:08,133
boundary past three, releasing the saved
copies. The number still means

84
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:13,817
the next packet wanted. Our convention stays
consistent through both failure

85
00:08:13,817 --> 00:08:19,500
and recovery.

86
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:25,083
Compare windows of one, four, and twenty-one.
More outstanding packets can

87
00:08:25,083 --> 00:08:30,667
fill the waiting time, but can't exceed the
link's capacity. TCP

88
00:08:30,667 --> 00:08:36,250
uses related ideas with byte numbers and
additional mechanisms beyond this

89
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:41,833
simplified packet model.

90
00:08:41,833 --> 00:08:47,625
Reliability emerged from a few pieces working
together: confirmations, saved copies,

91
00:08:47,625 --> 00:08:53,417
timers, sequence numbers, and a moving window.
Each solves a different

92
00:08:53,417 --> 00:08:59,208
failure. Together, they let communication
recover when the path can eventually

93
00:08:59,208 --> 00:09:05,000
carry data.
